\p{Research snapshot: arena commit \code{\verb~|99195b6b~} (2026-11-23, the commit the live site was built from), \code{\verb~|results.json~} of the same commit (37 checkers, 270 tests, 9990 results, runs dated 2026-11-14 … 2026-11-23). Paths are relative to \code{\verb~|refs/~} unless absolute.}

\p{Sources read (all read-only; nothing from the cloned repositories was built or executed — the only programs run were the host's official Lean 4.36.0 toolchain binaries \code{\verb~|leanexport~}, \code{\verb~|lean~}, \code{\verb~|leanchecker~}, and analysis scripts written for this report):}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena/~} (README.md, lka.py, set-timeouts.py, schemas/, checkers/*.yaml, tests/**, tutorial/**, .github/workflows/build-and-deploy.yml, flake.nix)}
\li{\code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena-history/~} — blob-less clone made for this report, only to read commit messages (7e43d5c, 8253be0)}
\li{\code{\verb~|lean4export/~} — standalone exporter, HEAD df81913 (toolchain v4.36.0-rc1)}
\li{\code{\verb~|lean4-leanexport/~} — sparse clone of leanprover/lean4 at tag v4.36.0 (611c117b…) made for this report: \code{\verb~|src/LeanExport/{Basic,Parse}.lean~}, \code{\verb~|src/Lean/Data/Json/*~}; other files read with \code{\verb~|git show HEAD:<path>~} (\code{\verb~|src/Lean/Replay.lean~}, \code{\verb~|src/Lean/Declaration.lean~}, \code{\verb~|src/Lean/Expr.lean~})}
\li{\code{\verb~|lean4/~} (another agent's sparse clone, v4.36.0): \code{\verb~|src/LeanChecker.lean~}, \code{\verb~|src/kernel/type_checker.cpp~}, \code{\verb~|src/kernel/inductive.cpp~}}
\li{Downloaded data in \code{\verb~|work/arena-site/~}: \code{\verb~|index.html~}, \code{\verb~|results.json~}, \code{\verb~|lean-arena-tests.tar.gz~}, extracted \code{\verb~|tests/{good,bad}/…~} (230 files, 52 MB)}
\li{Generated locally with the host's official Lean 4.36.0 toolchain (\code{\verb!!|~/.elan/toolchains/leanprover--lean4---v4.36.0/bin/leanexport!!}): \code{\verb~|work/arena-site/generated/init.ndjson~} and \code{\verb~|std.ndjson~}. Both are \strong{byte-identical to the arena tests \code{\verb~|init~} and \code{\verb~|std~}} (sha256 equal to the values in results.json), see §5.}
}

\subtree{
\title{0. Decision-relevant facts first}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Only instructions on \code{\verb~|mathlib~} rank speed.} Sort key = (bad tests accepted, good tests rejected, mathlib instructions \verb~|[~only if mathlib was \em{accepted}\verb~|]~, declined count) — \code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena/lka.py:2728-2738~}. Declines, crashes and timeouts never count as a wrong answer, only in the last tie-breaker.}
\li{\strong{mathlib export = 6,326,141,213 bytes, 112,081,718 lines}: 107,037,712 expression records, 4,273,914 name records, 48,246 level records, 721,845 declaration records (739,167 constants). Leader nano-lean spends 394.9 G instructions on it = \strong{62 instr/byte = 3.5 k instr/line}, parsing included. Every instruction per input byte costs 6.3 G.}
\li{\strong{Exporter output is byte-canonical}: one compact JSON object per line, keys in ASCII-sorted order, no whitespace, \code{\verb~|\n~} line ends, dense ids in emission order, children before parents. Verified: 100 \% of the 17,106,803 lines of \code{\verb~|init~}+\code{\verb~|std~} and 99.97 \% of the lines of the 230 tarball files match fixed byte templates (§2.3). The 271 exceptions are all in 8 hand-crafted \em{bad} tests (other key order, spaces after \code{\verb~|:~}/\code{\verb~|,~}, \code{\verb~|\u03b1~} escapes) → \strong{template fast path + generic JSON fallback per line}.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|app~} records are ~80 \% of all lines} (init 80.5 \%, std 76.2 \%), 48 bytes each; \code{\verb~|lam~} 8–10 \%, \code{\verb~|forallE~} 5–6 \%, names 4.6–5.5 \%, everything else < 2.5 \%. Declaration lines are < 1 \% of lines.}
\li{\strong{The expression table is already hash-consed} by the exporter modulo binder names/binder info (\code{\verb~|HashMap Expr Nat~} with \code{\verb~|BEq Expr = Expr.eqv~}): 0 structural duplicates among 16.07 M expr records of init+std and in every tarball file. No hash-consing is needed at load time for exporter output; id equality ⇒ structural equality (the converse must not be relied on for hand-crafted files).}
\li{\strong{~80 \% of expression records are reachable only from theorem \em{values} (proofs)}; ~94 \% are never referenced after the declaration that introduced them; 83–84 \% have exactly one referrer (§2.12).}
\li{\strong{Ids must not be assumed dense/ordered}: two \em{accept} tests use sparse and out-of-order ids (\code{\verb~|other/sparse-name-index~}, \code{\verb~|other/level-index-out-of-order~}). Ids are arbitrary naturals; "defined before use, bound once" is the only rule the official reader enforces.}
\li{\strong{Nothing derived may be trusted}: constructor records, recursor records (type, rules, \code{\verb~|nfields~}, \code{\verb~|k~}, counts), \code{\verb~|numFields~}, \code{\verb~|isRec~}, quot types, level-param lists. 16 of the 32 \code{\verb~|bugs/~} rejects are forged derived data (§3.3).}
\li{\strong{File order is dependency order} for exporter output (every constant declared before any record of another declaration uses it; verified on init, std, all good tests). The official checker itself replays by dependency, not file order, and accepts a file whose declaration lines are out of order (verified by experiment, §2.11).}
\li{\strong{Axioms}: accept every well-typed axiom. \code{\verb~|cedar~} contains 9 \code{\verb~|…_native.native_decide.ax_N_M~} axioms plus \code{\verb~|sorryAx~}; all top checkers and the official one accept it; only the con-leche family declines. \code{\verb~|Lean.trustCompiler~}/\code{\verb~|Lean.ofReduceBool~}/\code{\verb~|Lean.reduceBool~}/\code{\verb~|Lean.reduceNat~} \strong{do not occur at all} in the 4.36.0 \code{\verb~|init~}/\code{\verb~|std~} exports and the 4.36.0 C++ kernel has no \code{\verb~|reduce_native~} any more (only \code{\verb~|reduce_nat~}).}
\li{\strong{unsafe/partial}: never present in the real corpora; only \code{\verb~|tutorial/140~}, \code{\verb~|141~} (exported with \code{\verb~|--export-unsafe~}). Official behaviour: skip such constants, so anything using them fails with "unknown constant" → reject.}
\li{\strong{Timeouts} are wall-clock per test (mathlib 4120 s, perf tests 40–360 s, small tests none); a timeout is scored like a decline. Runner: 8 vCPU / 16 GB x86-64, one checker per VM, tests run sequentially.}
\li{\strong{JVM baseline measured in the arena} (tenet-jvm, plain \code{\verb~|java -XX:+UseParallelGC -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75~}): 0.546 G instructions / 64 MB / 75 ms for a trivial test; mathlib 9798 G instructions, 11.8 GB RSS, 1060 s wall.}
\li{Local ground truth exists: \code{\verb!!|~/.elan/toolchains/leanprover--lean4---v4.36.0/bin/leanchecker --from-export FILE!!} (exit 0/1) runs on this aarch64 host; its messages for all 92 bad tests are in §3.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. The arena contract}

\subtree{
\title{1.1 Checker definition, build, run}

\p{\code{\verb~|checkers/<name>.yaml~} (schema \code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena/schemas/checker.json~}): \code{\verb~|language~} (enum incl. \code{\verb~|java~}), \code{\verb~|url~}+\code{\verb~|ref~}+\code{\verb~|rev~} (git, fetched with \code{\verb~|git fetch --depth 1 origin <rev>~}), \code{\verb~|build~} (shell, optional), \code{\verb~|run~} (shell, required), optional \code{\verb~|version~}, \code{\verb~|timeout~} (s, min with the test's), \code{\verb~|declines~} (test names recorded as declined without running), \code{\verb~|threads~} (declared worker count; \strong{not read by lka.py or the templates at this commit} — informational), \code{\verb~|disable~}.}

\ul{
\li{Build: \code{\verb~|build~} runs via shell in the repo checkout \code{\verb~|_build/checkers/<name>/src~} (\code{\verb~|lka.py:1612-1652~}). Build time is not measured. Precedents for heavy builds: nano-lean \code{\verb~|scripts/pgo.sh~}, gruszka/pestka PGO builds trained on a downloaded Init export with \code{\verb~|sha256sum -c~}, kefir-wasm installs a rustup toolchain with curl, tenet-jvm \code{\verb~|make~} with the flake's \code{\verb~|jdk~}.}
\li{Tools on the runner come from \code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena/flake.nix~} (nixpkgs \code{\verb~|nixos-26.05~}): python3, elan, rustc, cargo, llvm 21, perf, cmake, jq, just, pypy, nodejs, binaryen, ocaml, opam, gmp, zig, ghc, texlive, dotnet-sdk_10, \strong{\code{\verb~|jdk~}} (version = nixpkgs default; not verified). No GraalVM: RLVM's build step has to fetch a pinned GraalVM itself (or the flake must be extended in the PR).}
\li{Run: \code{\verb~|sh -c "<run>"~} (not bash), cwd = \code{\verb~|_build/checkers/<name>/src~}, environment = runner environment + \code{\verb~|IN=<absolute path of the .ndjson>~} (\code{\verb~|lka.py:1750-1769~}). For cached tests \code{\verb~|$IN~} is a symlink into a cache volume (\code{\verb~|lka.py:1254-1257~}).}
\li{stdout/stderr are piped and drained; only the first and last 100 lines / 8 KiB of each are kept (\code{\verb~|lka.py:284-285~}, \code{\verb~|BoundedOutput~}). Output never blocks, but printing costs instructions.}
\li{\strong{Exit codes} (\code{\verb~|lka.py:1771-1781~}): 0 → \code{\verb~|accepted~}, 1 → \code{\verb~|rejected~}, 2 → \code{\verb~|declined~}, anything else (incl. signals, e.g. 137 = OOM kill) → \code{\verb~|error~}; killed by the arena on timeout → \code{\verb~|timeout~}.}
\li{Correctness (\code{\verb~|lka.py:1784-1798~}): declined → \code{\verb~|declined~}; error/timeout → \code{\verb~|error~}; test outcome \code{\verb~|either~} → \code{\verb~|either~}; match → \code{\verb~|correct~}; else \code{\verb~|incorrect~}.}
\li{Timeout handling: the whole wrapped command runs in a new session and the \strong{process group gets SIGKILL} when \code{\verb~|min(test.timeout, checker.timeout)~} wall seconds elapse (\code{\verb~|lka.py:365-441~}, \code{\verb~|1765-1766~}). No grace period, no partial credit.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.2 Measurement}

\p{Command actually executed (\code{\verb~|lka.py:496-514~}):}

\pre{\verb~|
perf stat -j -o <tmp.perf> -e duration_time -e task-clock -e instructions -- \
  time -f "real_seconds=%e\nuser_seconds=%U\nsys_seconds=%S\nmax_rss_kb=%M" -o <tmp.time> -- \
  sh -c "<run command>"
~}

\p{with \code{\verb~|LC_ALL=C~}.}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|instructions~} → result field \code{\verb~|instructions~}; \code{\verb~|task-clock~} → \code{\verb~|cpu_time~}; \code{\verb~|duration_time~} → \code{\verb~|wall_time~}; GNU time \code{\verb~|%M~} × 1024 → \code{\verb~|max_rss~} (\code{\verb~|lka.py:532-593~}). \code{\verb~|user_seconds~}/\code{\verb~|sys_seconds~} are written by \code{\verb~|time~} but not stored.}
\li{\code{\verb~|perf stat~} counts the command \strong{and everything it forks and all their threads} (default inherit; no \code{\verb~|--no-inherit~}). A wrapper script, a converter subprocess (gruszka/pestka run \code{\verb~|susz pack~} first), GC threads and JIT compiler threads are all inside the count.}
\li{\strong{User vs kernel instructions.} Documented intent: user-space only. The parser strips the \code{\verb~|:u~} suffix that perf appends when \code{\verb~|perf_event_paranoid=2~} forces user-only counting (\code{\verb~|lka.py:541-543~}), the workflow comment says "user-space-only at paranoid=2" (\code{\verb~|.github/workflows/build-and-deploy.yml:84-85~}), and commit 7e43d5c states "per-process user-space counting works at the default paranoid = 2 with no sysctl needed". \strong{Not verified:} the same job first runs a probe step that executes \code{\verb~|sudo sysctl -w kernel.perf_event_paranoid=-1~} (\code{\verb~|build-and-deploy.yml:146~}, \code{\verb~|continue-on-error~}); if sudo is permitted on the Namespace runner, the later \code{\verb~|lka.py run~} step counts user+kernel. The CI logs are not publicly downloadable (HTTP 403). Treat kernel-mode work (page faults of a multi-GB heap, \code{\verb~|read~}, \code{\verb~|madvise~}) as possibly charged.}
\li{\code{\verb~|max_rss~} is \code{\verb~|ru_maxrss~} of the child tree as reported by GNU time: the largest single process, not a sum (Linux \code{\verb~|getrusage~} semantics; not re-verified on the runner). Memory is displayed, not ranked. The hard limit is the 16 GB VM: rpylean was killed with exit 137 at 14.65 GB on \code{\verb~|perf/magma-string-pair-n9~} → \code{\verb~|error~}.}
\li{\strong{Virtual time} = \code{\verb~|instructions / 6e9~} (\code{\verb~|lka.py:2339-2345~}, constant at \code{\verb~|2687~}). The leaderboard's ⏱️ column and the third sort key use the instruction count only. Cache misses, waiting and I/O time are free; instructions are everything (nano-lean retires 3.2 G instr per CPU-second on mathlib, tenet-jvm 8.6 G, official 5.4 G).}
\li{Fallback if perf is missing: wall time only, \code{\verb~|instructions = 0~} (\code{\verb~|lka.py:599-613~}).}
\li{Each (checker, test) pair is measured \strong{once}; results are reused across site builds while the source hashes of checker and test are unchanged (\code{\verb~|ci-select~}, \code{\verb~|lka.py:3086-3152~}). No averaging.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.3 Scoring and ranking}

\p{\code{\verb~|compute_checker_stats~} (\code{\verb~|lka.py:2038-2141~}) and \code{\verb~|sort_key~} (\code{\verb~|lka.py:2728-2738~}):}

\pre{\verb~|
for each test result of the checker:
    if status in (declined, error, timeout): declined_count += 1; continue      # never counts as wrong
    if outcome == either: continue                                              # never counts
    if outcome == accept: accept_total += 1; accept_correct += (status == accepted)
    if outcome == reject: reject_total += 1; reject_correct += (status == rejected)
    if test == "mathlib" and status == accepted: mathlib_instructions = instructions
key = (reject_total - reject_correct,          # bad proofs accepted
       accept_total - accept_correct,          # good proofs rejected
       mathlib_instructions or +inf,           # 0/missing sorts last
       declined_count)
~}

\p{Consequences: (a) when unsure, exit 2 (or crash) rather than guess — a decline costs only the last tie-breaker; (b) rejecting mathlib, or timing out on it, removes the checker from the speed ranking; (c) \code{\verb~|either~} tests are free.}

\p{Leaderboard at this snapshot (columns: wrongly accepted / wrongly rejected / declined(decl,err,timeout) / mathlib virtual s / G instr / wall s / cpu s / RSS GB; \code{\verb~|thr~} = declared threads):}

