\tr{\td{init}\td{347,772,063}\td{6,495,296}\td{299,085}\td{571}\td{6,137,092}\td{58,547}\td{59,979}\td{4/15,239/42,451/244/4/605}\td{4.36.0}\td{\code{\verb~|import Init~}}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{19,031,110}\td{361,766}\td{30,662}\td{73}\td{326,617}\td{4,413}\td{4,846}\td{3/1,687/2,532/2/4/185}\td{4.34.1}\td{6 \code{\verb~|decide +kernel~} theorems + deps}}
\tr{\td{init-prelude}\td{3,811,816}\td{65,406}\td{7,491}\td{113}\td{55,961}\td{1,840}\td{2,121}\td{3/1,536/161/10/4/126}\td{4.36.0}\td{\code{\verb~|prelude~} + \code{\verb~|import Init.Prelude~}}}
}

\p{Axioms declared: mathlib, cslib, std, init: \code{\verb~|propext~}, \code{\verb~|Quot.sound~}, \code{\verb~|Classical.choice~}, \code{\verb~|sorryAx~}; init-prelude: \code{\verb~|Quot.sound~}, \code{\verb~|Classical.choice~}, \code{\verb~|sorryAx~}; navier/con-leche/reachability: the three standard ones; cedar: the three standard ones, \code{\verb~|sorryAx~}, and 9 \code{\verb~|_private.Cedar.….<thm>._native.native_decide.ax_N_M~}. Average 53–57 bytes/line everywhere. Virtual seconds (instr/6e9) of leaders, same order as the table rows:}

\table{
\tr{\th{test}\th{nano-lean}\th{gruszka}\th{sokonanoda}\th{rpylean}\th{pestka}\th{kefir}\th{tenet-jvm}\th{official}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{65.8}\td{77.0}\td{87.9}\td{96.3}\td{245.9}\td{429.5}\td{1633.1}\td{2015.9}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{23.6}\td{27.4}\td{32.0}\td{35.4}\td{79.0}\td{141.1}\td{396.6}\td{566.1}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{17.2}\td{21.8}\td{40.3}\td{24.1}\td{57.4}\td{130.7}\td{493.2}\td{671.9}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{10.5}\td{10.3}\td{12.0}\td{13.3}\td{26.1}\td{46.2}\td{79.2}\td{147.2}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{10.3}\td{9.2}\td{11.5}\td{11.9}\td{20.3}\td{47.2}\td{120.6}\td{216.2}}
\tr{\td{std}\td{6.7}\td{6.7}\td{8.1}\td{9.2}\td{18.0}\td{32.4}\td{60.2}\td{110.9}}
\tr{\td{init}\td{4.1}\td{4.1}\td{4.8}\td{5.4}\td{10.9}\td{18.2}\td{37.3}\td{66.9}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{1.9}\td{2.4}\td{4.3}\td{4.3}\td{4.4}\td{3.4}\td{21.4}\td{23.5}}
}

\p{nano-lean is at 3.2–4.9 k instructions per export line on the seven large corpora (mathlib 3.5 k, cslib 3.2 k, init/std 3.8 k, con-leche 4.9 k). Other parse-time evidence: lazylean (C++) loads mathlib in 12.8 s wall; con-leche (Lean) "parse 19.8 s, check 153.0 s (4 workers)"; nanobruijn reports "107,037,712 parser entries → 97,515,217 DAG entries" after its own shift-normalising interning. Slowest single mathlib declaration for con-leche: \code{\verb~|_private.Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.EllipticCurve.Affine.AddSubMap.0.WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point.sym2x_add_sub_eq_addSubMap_sym2x._proof_1_8~} (3.05 s); lean4lean needs 48.8 s for \code{\verb~|IntermediateField.algHomAdjoinIntegralEquiv_symm_apply_gen~}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. The export format (format version 3.1.0) as actually produced}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 Which exporter}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|tests/lean-toolchain~} = \code{\verb~|leanprover/lean4:v4.36.0~}. Since Lean v4.35.0 the toolchain ships a \code{\verb~|leanexport~} binary and the arena uses it (\code{\verb~|lka.py:752-758~}, \code{\verb~|1390-1403~}); only older toolchains build \code{\verb~|leanprover/lean4export~} master.}
\li{Bundled source: \code{\verb~|lean4-leanexport/src/LeanExport/Basic.lean~} (writer), \code{\verb~|…/Parse.lean~} (reader used by \code{\verb~|leanchecker --from-export~}), \code{\verb~|lean4/src/LeanExport.lean~} (main). It is the standalone \code{\verb~|lean4export/Export.lean~} with one semantic difference: standalone HEAD skips \code{\verb~|partial~} definitions unless \code{\verb~|--export-unsafe~} (\code{\verb~|lean4export/Export.lean:238~}), bundled 4.36.0 skips only \code{\verb~|isUnsafe~} ones (\code{\verb~|Basic.lean:255~}). No visible effect on the corpora (§2.7).}
\li{Invocation: \code{\verb~|lake env leanexport <Module> [-- <decl>…] > out.ndjson~} (\code{\verb~|lka.py:776-781~}); no options, so \code{\verb~|Expr.mdata~} is stripped and unsafe constants are skipped. \code{\verb~|leanfile~} tests compile the file as module \code{\verb~|Test~} in a throw-away lake project and export \code{\verb~|Test~}. Tutorial tests use their own driver around the same writer with \code{\verb~|--export-unsafe~} and optional renaming (\code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena/tutorial/Tutorial/Export.lean:127-176~}).}
\li{Header metadata of every test: exporter \code{\verb~|lean4export~} 3.1.0 (2 files: \code{\verb~|handcrafted~} 0.1.0), format 3.1.0. Lean versions seen: 4.36.0 (most), 4.35.0, 4.34.1, 4.34.0-rc2, 4.29.1; githash is a real hash, forty zeros, or the string \code{\verb~|generated~}.}
\li{The output is deterministic and platform independent: generated on this aarch64 host it is byte-identical to the arena's x86-64 exports (§5).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 File structure}

\pre{\verb~|
line 1      {"meta":{"exporter":{"name":S,"version":S},"format":{"version":S},"lean":{"githash":S,"version":S}}}
lines 2..n  one record per line: name | level | expr | declaration
~}

\ul{
\li{Encoding UTF-8, every line ends with a single \code{\verb~|\n~} (no CRLF, no blank lines, final newline present) — verified on all files.}
\li{Three independent id spaces: names (\code{\verb~|in~}), levels (\code{\verb~|il~}), expressions (\code{\verb~|ie~}). Implicit entries: \strong{name 0 = anonymous}, \strong{level 0 = zero}; neither is ever emitted. Expression ids start at 0.}
\li{Written by \code{\verb~|IO.println (json.compress)~}; \code{\verb~|Json.mkObj~} builds a \code{\verb~|Std.TreeMap String Json~}, so \strong{keys of every object are emitted in ascending code-point order} (\code{\verb~|lean4-leanexport/src/Lean/Data/Json/Basic.lean:230-231~}, \code{\verb~|Printer.lean:167-232~}). That is why the id key is not in a fixed position: \code{\verb~|{"app":…,"ie":N}~} but \code{\verb~|{"ie":N,"lam":…}~}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.3 Canonical byte templates (the fast path)}

\p{\code{\verb~|N~} = decimal natural without leading zeros; \code{\verb~|L~} = \code{\verb~|[]~} or \code{\verb~|[N,N,…]~}; \code{\verb~|S~} = JSON string as in §2.8; \code{\verb~|B~} = \code{\verb~|true|false~}. These regular shapes matched every line of init+std and of all exporter-generated test files.}

\pre{\verb~|
names   {"in":N,"num":{"i":N,"pre":N}}                                          Name.num pre i
        {"in":N,"str":{"pre":N,"str":S}}                                        Name.str pre s
levels  {"il":N,"imax":[N,N]}   {"il":N,"max":[N,N]}   {"il":N,"param":N}   {"il":N,"succ":N}
                                                       (param → NAME id; others → LEVEL ids)
exprs   {"app":{"arg":N,"fn":N},"ie":N}                                         note: arg before fn
        {"bvar":N,"ie":N}                                                       de Bruijn index
        {"const":{"name":N,"us":L},"ie":N}                                      name id, LEVEL ids
        {"forallE":{"binderInfo":BI,"body":N,"name":N,"type":N},"ie":N}
        {"ie":N,"lam":{"binderInfo":BI,"body":N,"name":N,"type":N}}
        {"ie":N,"letE":{"body":N,"name":N,"nondep":B,"type":N,"value":N}}
        {"ie":N,"natVal":"<decimal digits>"}                                    string, any length
        {"ie":N,"proj":{"idx":N,"struct":N,"typeName":N}}                       field index, expr id, name id
        {"ie":N,"sort":N}                                                       level id
        {"ie":N,"strVal":S}
        {"ie":N,"mdata":{"data":{…},"expr":N}}                                  only with --export-mdata (never in the arena)
        BI = "default" | "implicit" | "strictImplicit" | "instImplicit"
decls   {"axiom":{"isUnsafe":B,"levelParams":L,"name":N,"type":N}}
        {"def":{"all":L,"hints":H,"levelParams":L,"name":N,"safety":"safe"|"unsafe"|"partial","type":N,"value":N}}
              H = "abbrev" | "opaque" | {"regular":N}
        {"opaque":{"all":L,"isUnsafe":B,"levelParams":L,"name":N,"type":N,"value":N}}
        {"quot":{"kind":"type"|"ctor"|"lift"|"ind","levelParams":L,"name":N,"type":N}}
        {"thm":{"all":L,"levelParams":L,"name":N,"type":N,"value":N}}
        {"inductive":{"ctors":[C,…],"recs":[R,…],"types":[T,…]}}                 note: ctors, recs BEFORE types
          C = {"cidx":N,"induct":N,"isUnsafe":B,"levelParams":L,"name":N,"numFields":N,"numParams":N,"type":N}
          R = {"all":L,"isUnsafe":B,"k":B,"levelParams":L,"name":N,"numIndices":N,"numMinors":N,
               "numMotives":N,"numParams":N,"rules":[{"ctor":N,"nfields":N,"rhs":N},…],"type":N}
          T = {"all":L,"ctors":L,"isRec":B,"isReflexive":B,"isUnsafe":B,"levelParams":L,"name":N,
               "numIndices":N,"numNested":N,"numParams":N,"type":N}
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|levelParams~}, \code{\verb~|all~}, \code{\verb~|ctors~} (inside T), \code{\verb~|induct~}, rule \code{\verb~|ctor~}, \code{\verb~|typeName~}, binder \code{\verb~|name~} are \strong{name ids}; \code{\verb~|us~}, \code{\verb~|sort~}, \code{\verb~|succ~}, \code{\verb~|max~}, \code{\verb~|imax~} operands are \strong{level ids}; \code{\verb~|type~}, \code{\verb~|value~}, \code{\verb~|body~}, \code{\verb~|fn~}, \code{\verb~|arg~}, \code{\verb~|struct~}, \code{\verb~|rhs~}, mdata \code{\verb~|expr~} are \strong{expression ids}.}

\p{First-bytes dispatch (after \code{\verb~|{"~}): \code{\verb~|ap~}→app, \code{\verb~|ax~}→axiom, \code{\verb~|b~}→bvar, \code{\verb~|c~}→const, \code{\verb~|d~}→def, \code{\verb~|f~}→forallE, \code{\verb~|ie~}→ie-first expr (then the second key: \code{\verb~|lam~},\code{\verb~|letE~},\code{\verb~|natVal~},\code{\verb~|proj~},\code{\verb~|sort~},\code{\verb~|strVal~},\code{\verb~|mdata~}), \code{\verb~|il~}→level, \code{\verb~|in"~}→name, \code{\verb~|ind~}→inductive, \code{\verb~|m~}→meta, \code{\verb~|o~}→opaque, \code{\verb~|q~}→quot, \code{\verb~|t~}→thm.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.4 Id spaces: what holds and what may be assumed}

\p{Writer logic (\code{\verb~|Basic.lean:100-110~}): look the object up in a \code{\verb~|HashMap~}; if absent, first emit its children (recursively), then assign \code{\verb~|idx := map.size~} and print. Hence for exporter output:}

\table{
\tr{\th{property}\th{exporter output}\th{hand-crafted tests}\th{official reader (\code{\verb~|Parse.lean~})}}
\tr{\td{ids dense, starting at 1 (names, levels) / 0 (exprs), strictly +1 in file order}\td{yes (verified: init, std, 228 of 230 tarball files)}\td{\strong{no} — \code{\verb~|other/sparse-name-index~} (name id 2 without 1, expr id 4 without 0–3), \code{\verb~|other/level-index-out-of-order~} (level 2 defined before level 1); both are \em{accept} tests}\td{ids are keys of \code{\verb~|HashMap Nat _~}; any natural}}
\tr{\td{every referenced id is defined on an earlier line}\td{yes, 0 forward references anywhere}\td{yes in all tests}\td{required: "Name/Level/Expr not found" otherwise}}
\tr{\td{an id is bound once}\td{yes}\td{yes}\td{required: "index N bound twice" (\code{\verb~|Parse.lean:61-86~})}}
\tr{\td{no two ids denote the same object}\td{names: unique by structure; levels: unique by syntax (\code{\verb~|max u v~} ≠ \code{\verb~|max v u~}); exprs: unique modulo binder names and binderInfo}\td{not guaranteed}\td{not required}}
\tr{\td{largest ids}\td{mathlib: expr 107,037,711; name 4,273,914; level 48,246 → all fit in int32}\td{tiny}\td{unbounded \code{\verb~|Nat~}}}
}

\p{Recommended contract: fast path asserts \code{\verb~|id == next++~} per space; on the first violation switch that space to a sparse table (id → slot; grow an array while the id is small, else a hash map); a reference to an undefined id or a rebinding = malformed input (official: uncaught exception, exit 1 = \em{rejected}).}

\p{Because expression lookup is by \code{\verb~|BEq Expr = Expr.eqv~} ("alpha equivalent, binder annotations ignored", \code{\verb~|Expr.lean:803-811~}), the record of a binder shows the binder name/info of its \em{first} occurrence only. \code{\verb~|letE.nondep~} is normalised to \code{\verb~|false~} before lookup (\code{\verb~|Basic.lean:158-164~}); all 5072+33,496 letE records seen have \code{\verb~|nondep:false~}. Binder names, binderInfo and \code{\verb~|nondep~} are irrelevant to checking and need not be stored.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.5 Names, levels, expressions}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Names} are hierarchical: \code{\verb~|str(pre, s)~} or \code{\verb~|num(pre, i)~}. A dot inside \code{\verb~|s~} is part of the component (\code{\verb~|str(0,"a.b")~} ≠ \code{\verb~|a~} then \code{\verb~|b~}); never identify names by a dotted rendering. \code{\verb~|i~} is a natural: observed up to 4,283,319,217 (> 2³¹; 9–10-digit values are common: 9687 ten-digit values in the tarball) → parse as \code{\verb~|long~}, with an overflow path. Longest component seen: 230 chars. In hand-crafted files two ids may denote the same name, so constants, level parameters and special names must be resolved by structural (interned) name, not by raw id.}
\li{\strong{Levels}: \code{\verb~|succ~}, \code{\verb~|max~}, \code{\verb~|imax~}, \code{\verb~|param(name)~}; \code{\verb~|zero~} only as id 0; \code{\verb~|mvar~} never. Level ids are deduplicated syntactically only. \code{\verb~|const.us~} lists level ids, one per universe parameter (must equal the constant's arity — \code{\verb~|bugs/constlevels~}, \code{\verb~|large-elim-*~}).}
\li{\strong{Expressions}: \code{\verb~|bvar~} (loose indices can legally appear in records, since subterms under binders are separate records; a declaration root must be closed — \code{\verb~|bugs/loose-bvar-value~}), \code{\verb~|sort~}, \code{\verb~|const~}, \code{\verb~|app~} (binary), \code{\verb~|lam~}, \code{\verb~|forallE~}, \code{\verb~|letE~}, \code{\verb~|proj(typeName, idx, struct)~}, \code{\verb~|natVal~}, \code{\verb~|strVal~}, \code{\verb~|mdata~} (never emitted without the flag; the official reader maps it to an empty-metadata \code{\verb~|mdata~} node). \code{\verb~|fvar~}/\code{\verb~|mvar~} cannot be exported (\code{\verb~|panic!~}).}
\li{Before the first \code{\verb~|natVal~} the writer exports constant \code{\verb~|Nat~} (if not yet exported); before the first \code{\verb~|strVal~} it exports \code{\verb~|Char.ofNat~} and \code{\verb~|String.ofList~} (\code{\verb~|Basic.lean:234-244~}). So a declaration block can be emitted \em{in the middle of} the expression records of the declaration being written; "the records between two declaration lines belong to the second" is not exact.}
\li{\strong{Nat literals}: decimal string, arbitrary length; the official reader uses \code{\verb~|String.toNat?~} (\code{\verb~|Parse.lean:237-241~}). Longest in the available files: 20 digits (2⁶⁴ in \code{\verb~|bugs/nat-shift-huge~}); mathlib not inspected (must go through a bignum path beyond 18 digits). The 4.36 kernel refuses numerals above 128 MB (\code{\verb~|LEAN_NAT_MAX_SIZE~}, \code{\verb~|lean4/src/kernel/type_checker.cpp:30-36~}, checked in \code{\verb~|infer_lit~} and after \code{\verb~|Nat.succ/add/sub/mul/pow/shiftLeft~}), and requires the second argument of \code{\verb~|Nat.pow~}/\code{\verb~|Nat.shiftLeft~} to fit in 32 bits unless the result is trivially 0 (\code{\verb~|type_checker.cpp:610-660~}).}
\li{\strong{String literals} denote lists of Unicode scalar values: the kernel expands \code{\verb~|"…"~} to \code{\verb~|String.ofList (List.cons.{0} Char (Char.ofNat n₁) … (List.nil.{0} Char))~} with one \code{\verb~|Char.ofNat~} per \strong{code point} (\code{\verb~|lean4/src/kernel/inductive.cpp:1342-1390~}, \code{\verb~|type_checker.cpp:1100-1103~}). \code{\verb~|bugs/str-lit-utf8~} (accept) and \code{\verb~|bugs/str-lit-utf8-bad~} (reject) test 2-, 3- and 4-byte characters. In Java: iterate code points, never \code{\verb~|char~}s or bytes.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.6 Declarations}

\p{Field meaning = the fields of \code{\verb~|Lean.ConstantInfo~} (\code{\verb~|Declaration.lean~}); who must believe what is in §2.9.}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|axiom~}: name, levelParams, type, isUnsafe.}
\li{\code{\verb~|def~}: name, levelParams, type, value, \code{\verb~|hints~} (\code{\verb~|"opaque"~}, \code{\verb~|"abbrev"~}, \code{\verb~|{"regular":h}~} with \code{\verb~|h~} a UInt32 height; max seen 36), \code{\verb~|safety~}, \code{\verb~|all~} (names of the mutual block as elaborated; informational; length ≠ 1 for 4 defs in init, 6 in std).}
\li{\code{\verb~|thm~}: name, levelParams, type, value, all.}
\li{\code{\verb~|opaque~}: name, levelParams, type, value, isUnsafe, all (the official reader tolerates a missing \code{\verb~|isUnsafe~}, default false).}
\li{\code{\verb~|quot~}: always the four records \code{\verb~|Quot~} (kind \code{\verb~|type~}), \code{\verb~|Quot.mk~} (\code{\verb~|ctor~}), \code{\verb~|Quot.lift~} (\code{\verb~|lift~}), \code{\verb~|Quot.ind~} (\code{\verb~|ind~}) in this order, preceded by the block of \code{\verb~|Eq~} (\code{\verb~|Basic.lean:308-322~}).}
\li{\code{\verb~|inductive~}: one line per \em{mutual block}: all \code{\verb~|types~} in the order of \code{\verb~|all~}; all \code{\verb~|ctors~} grouped by type in that order, each type's constructors in \code{\verb~|cidx~} order; all recursors whose \code{\verb~|all~} contains the first type, \strong{in arbitrary order} (iteration of a \code{\verb~|NameSet~}: e.g. \code{\verb~|Lean.Syntax.rec_2, Lean.Syntax.rec, Lean.Syntax.rec_1~}; \code{\verb~|ImaxProp.rec~} before \code{\verb~|ImaxPropDummy.rec~}). Auxiliary recursors of nested inductives (\code{\verb~|X.rec_1~}, …) are in the block of \code{\verb~|X~}; their rules name constructors of other types (\code{\verb~|List.cons~}, \code{\verb~|Array.mk~}). Recursors have their own \code{\verb~|levelParams~} (motive universe first when large elimination is allowed). Match recursors and constructors by name, never by position.}
}

\p{Observed flag frequencies (init / std): k=true recursors 6 / 6; reflexive types 4 / 4; nested (\code{\verb~|numNested>0~}) 1 / 3; mutual blocks 0 / 0 (one in the tests: \code{\verb~|bugs/proj-of-imax-prop~}); \code{\verb~|isUnsafe~} always false; \code{\verb~|safety~} always \code{\verb~|safe~}; hints init: 7203 regular, 7884 abbrev, 152 opaque.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.7 Ordering guarantees}

\p{\code{\verb~|dumpConstant~} (\code{\verb~|Basic.lean:252-429~}) marks the constant visited, exports every constant used by its type and value (recursively), then writes its own names/levels/expressions and finally its declaration line. For an inductive block the dependencies of all types, constructors, recursor types and rule right-hand sides are exported before any record of the block.}

\ul{
\li{Verified with a script on init, std and every \em{good} tarball file: each constant referenced by a declaration is declared on an earlier line, except references inside one \code{\verb~|inductive~} line to members of the same block (types ↔ constructors ↔ recursors ↔ rules).}
\li{The only violations in the whole tarball are four \em{bad} tests: \code{\verb~|tutorial/014_selfProof~} (self reference), \code{\verb~|tutorial/140~}, \code{\verb~|141~} (unsafe/partial self-recursive definitions) and \code{\verb~|bugs/nested-name-capture~} (a recursor rule naming a constant that is declared later).}
\li{Top-level iteration order is that of \code{\verb~|env.constants.toList~} filtered by \code{\verb~|!isInternal~} (\code{\verb~|Basic.lean:466~}) — a hash-map order, not source order, but dependency-closed. With \code{\verb~|-- decls~} only those and their dependency cone are exported.}
\li{Partial definitions (\code{\verb~|safety: partial~}) would be exported by the bundled 4.36.0 writer if reachable, but they are only the \code{\verb~|_unsafe_rec~} auxiliaries (internal names, referenced only by each other). Evidence that the corpora contain none: 0 occurrences of \code{\verb~|"unsafe"~}/\code{\verb~|"partial"~}/\code{\verb~|_unsafe_rec~} in init and std; con-ron/con-agua/con-leche decline any such definition at once and accept all corpora but cedar; sokonanoda declines them and accepts cedar.}
}

\p{Checking strictly in file order with "unknown constant ⇒ reject" is therefore complete for all current accept tests; the official checker is more liberal (§2.11).}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.8 Strings, escapes, numbers}

\p{Writer (\code{\verb~|lean4-leanexport/src/Lean/Data/Json/Printer.lean:36-93~}):}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|"~} → \code{\verb~|\"~}, \code{\verb~|\~} → \code{\verb~|\\~}, U+000A → \code{\verb~|\n~}, U+000D → \code{\verb~|\r~}; any other code point < U+0020 → \code{\verb~|\u00XX~} (four lowercase hex digits); \strong{everything ≥ U+0020 is written raw as UTF-8}, including U+007F and all non-ASCII. No \code{\verb~|\t~}, \code{\verb~|\b~}, \code{\verb~|\f~}, \code{\verb~|\/~}, no surrogate escapes.}
\li{Observed in std (a superset of init): only \code{\verb~|\"~} (46), \code{\verb~|\\~} (45), \code{\verb~|\n~} (88), \code{\verb~|\r~} (42); raw non-ASCII on 1225 lines.}
\li{Numbers are plain decimal naturals (no sign, fraction or exponent). Booleans are \code{\verb~|true~}/\code{\verb~|false~}. \code{\verb~|null~} never occurs.}
}

\p{Hand-crafted files use ordinary JSON: \code{\verb~|\u03b1~} occurs in \code{\verb~|bugs/nested-name-capture~} (name components \code{\verb~|α~}, \code{\verb~|β~} written by an ASCII-escaping JSON library). The fallback parser must implement all JSON escapes including surrogate pairs; a lone surrogate cannot be a Lean string (reject).}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.9 What a checker must not trust}

\table{
\tr{\th{record / field}\th{status}\th{evidence}}
\tr{\td{constructor records (\code{\verb~|type~}, \code{\verb~|induct~}, \code{\verb~|cidx~}, \code{\verb~|numParams~}, \code{\verb~|numFields~}, levelParams)}\td{re-derive from the types' \code{\verb~|ctors~} lists and compare; reject any constructor record of a block that is not derived, or whose \code{\verb~|induct~} is not in the block}\td{\code{\verb~|bugs/ctor-num-fields~}, \code{\verb~|other/orphan-ctor~}, tutorial 134, 138, 139}}
\tr{\td{recursor records (name, levelParams, \code{\verb~|type~}, \code{\verb~|all~}, \code{\verb~|numParams/Indices/Motives/Minors~}, \code{\verb~|k~}, \code{\verb~|rules[].ctor/nfields/rhs~})}\td{re-derive completely; compare structurally (official: \code{\verb~|BEq~}, i.e. alpha-equivalence ignoring binder names/info), \strong{not} up to definitional equality; reject extra, misnamed or orphan recursors}\td{\code{\verb~|bugs/nat-rec-rules~}, \code{\verb~|unit-rec-rule~}, \code{\verb~|rec-rule-wrong-arg~}, \code{\verb~|rec-rule-proof-irrel~}, \code{\verb~|rec-rule-proof-irrel-nested~}, \code{\verb~|rec-type-proof-irrel~}, \code{\verb~|nested-rec-rule-nfields~}, \code{\verb~|rec-k-lie~}, \code{\verb~|nat-rec-k-lie~}, \code{\verb~|large-elim-param~}, \code{\verb~|large-elim-prop-bool~}, \code{\verb~|rec-missing-ih~}, \code{\verb~|orphan-rec~}, \code{\verb~|other/extra-rec~}, tutorial 073, 135–138}}
\tr{\td{inductive \code{\verb~|numIndices~}, \code{\verb~|isRec~}, \code{\verb~|isReflexive~}, \code{\verb~|numNested~}, \code{\verb~|isUnsafe~}}\td{recompute; the official checker never reads them from the export (Replay builds an \code{\verb~|inductDecl~} from names, types, constructor names/types, \code{\verb~|levelParams~} and \code{\verb~|numParams~} of the first type only)}\td{\code{\verb~|Replay.lean~} (\code{\verb~|.inductInfo~} case); \code{\verb~|corner-cases/vanishing-occurrence~} notes exported \code{\verb~|isRec = true~} although the kernel finds no occurrence}}
\tr{\td{inductive \code{\verb~|numParams~}, \code{\verb~|levelParams~}, \code{\verb~|all~}, \code{\verb~|ctors~}}\td{\em{inputs} of the declaration: validate (binder count ≥ numParams, no duplicate/undeclared universe names, constructors exist)}\td{tutorial 048–052, \code{\verb~|other/inductive-undeclared-universe~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|quot~} records}\td{check against the four canonical signatures, or generate them and compare; \code{\verb~|Eq~} must exist with the right shape}\td{\code{\verb~|bugs/quot-bad-type~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|levelParams~} of any declaration}\td{no duplicates; every \code{\verb~|param~} used in type/value must be listed}\td{tutorial 019, 048, \code{\verb~|other/inductive-undeclared-universe~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|hints~}}\td{untrusted performance hint; influences only the unfolding order (the official kernel takes it as given)}\td{—}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|all~} of def/thm/opaque, \code{\verb~|isUnsafe~}, binder names, binderInfo, \code{\verb~|nondep~}}\td{ignore}\td{—}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|safety~}}\td{\code{\verb~|unsafe~}/\code{\verb~|partial~} definitions must not be usable by safe declarations}\td{tutorial 140, 141}}
\tr{\td{name uniqueness across all declared constants, including generated \code{\verb~|X.rec~}, constructors, and any internal auxiliary names the checker invents}\td{reject duplicates; never let a later declaration capture an auxiliary name}\td{tutorial 132–139, \code{\verb~|bugs/nested-name-capture~}}}
\tr{\td{header \code{\verb~|meta~}}\td{the official reader discards line 1 unread (\code{\verb~|Parse.lean:511-512~}); use it only to decline unknown format versions}\td{—}}
}
}

