\subtree{
\title{2.11 The official reader and checker (\code{\verb~|leanchecker --from-export~})}

\p{\code{\verb~|lean4/src/LeanChecker.lean~} (\code{\verb~|checkExport~}), \code{\verb~|Parse.lean~}, \code{\verb~|Replay.lean~} (v4.36.0):}

\pre{\verb~|
parse:   skip line 1 without parsing it;
         for every further line: Json.parse (blank line = error); dispatch on the key set, any key order;
         unknown key set / wrong field type / undefined or rebound id / natVal not a digit string → exception;
         every constant (also each type, ctor, rec of a block) goes into constMap; a name seen twice →
         "Duplicate declaration"                                   -- uncaught exception ⇒ exit code 1
replay:  remaining := all constants that are neither unsafe nor partial; Quot.mk/lift/ind are removed
         replayConstant(c): if c ∈ remaining: move to pending; first replayConstant every constant used
                            by c's type/value (dependency order, NOT file order); then
           def / thm / axiom / opaque → kernel addDecl
           inductive → addDecl(inductDecl lparams nparams [{name,type,ctors:[{name,type}]} for all types in `all`])
                       (lparams, nparams from the first type reached; the kernel generates ctors and recursors)
           ctor, rec  → only remembered
           quot       → replayConstant `Eq; addDecl quotDecl
         at the end: every remembered ctor/rec must equal (BEq) the kernel-generated constant of that name,
                     else "Invalid constructor/recursor" or "No such constructor/recursor"
post:    Quot, Quot.mk, Quot.lift, Quot.ind, if present in the export, must equal the kernel's
exit 0 "Lean default kernel accepts the solution"; exit 1 on any kernel error or exception
~}

\p{Notes: unsafe/partial constants are simply absent, so their users fail with "unknown constant". Forged recursors are often rejected at their first \em{use} (wrong universe arity or type), because the comparison runs last.}

\p{Behaviour confirmed by experiment with the local 4.36.0 \code{\verb~|leanchecker~} on edited copies of tutorial tests (files in /tmp, not kept):}

\table{
\tr{\th{input change}\th{official result}}
\tr{\td{a theorem's declaration line moved to the end of the file, after the theorem that uses it}\td{\strong{accepted} (dependency replay; file order of declarations is irrelevant as long as ids are defined before use)}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|"recs":[]~} in the block of \code{\verb~|Empty~} while a later definition uses \code{\verb~|Empty.rec~}}\td{\strong{accepted} (the kernel-generated recursor exists whether or not the export lists it)}}
\tr{\td{garbage text as line 1}\td{accepted (line 1 is never parsed)}}
\tr{\td{no meta line (line 1 is the first record)}\td{exit 1, "Name not found 1" (the first record is swallowed)}}
\tr{\td{blank line in the middle}\td{exit 1, "Invalid JSON"}}
\tr{\td{missing final newline; CRLF line ends}\td{accepted}}
\tr{\td{the same name record twice (id rebound)}\td{exit 1, "Name index 1 bound twice"}}
}

\p{No arena test exercises the first two rows today (every good test is in dependency order and lists all recursors), but an exact emulation needs: (1) a constant table of the whole file before deciding "unknown constant" — on a reference to a constant declared later, check that one first (cycle ⇒ reject); a purely streaming checker should decline rather than reject in that situation; (2) generated constructors/recursors must be usable even when the export omits their records.}
}

