\subtree{
\title{2.13 Byte-level parsing advice}

\p{What a single-pass hand-written parser may assume \strong{on the fast path only} (and must verify byte-by-byte, falling back otherwise): fixed key order, no whitespace, \code{\verb~|\n~} terminators, decimal ids without leading zeros, \code{\verb~|id == next expected id~}, referenced ids \code{\verb~|< next~}. What it may never assume: density/order of ids, absence of escapes, key order, line length bounds (an \code{\verb~|inductive~} line grows with the block; allow arbitrarily long lines), 32-bit \code{\verb~|i~}/\code{\verb~|natVal~}/\code{\verb~|idx~}.}

\pre{\verb~|
buf: one reusable byte[] of a few MiB filled by read(); carry an incomplete tail line to the front.
     (A mapped 6.3 GB file would count towards max RSS while resident, as Linux counts file-backed pages; plain reads do not.)
per line, p at '{':
  switch on buf[p+2], buf[p+3]:
    "ap": match `{"app":{"arg":` A `,"fn":` F `},"ie":` N `}\n`      → 2 ints + check N == nextExpr
    "ie": match `{"ie":` N `,"`; then
            `lam":{"binderInfo":"` → skip to the closing quote; `,"body":` B `,"name":` (skip digits) `,"type":` T `}}\n`
            `letE":{"body":` B `,"name":` skip `,"nondep":` skip `,"type":` T `,"value":` V `}}\n`
            `proj":{"idx":` I `,"struct":` E `,"typeName":` P `}}\n`
            `sort":` L `}\n`      `natVal":"` digits `"}\n`      `strVal":` string `}\n`
    "fo": `{"forallE":{"binderInfo":"` skip `","body":` B `,"name":` skip `,"type":` T `},"ie":` N `}\n`
    "co": `{"const":{"name":` P `,"us":[` ints `]},"ie":` N `}\n`
    "bv": `{"bvar":` I `,"ie":` N `}\n`
    "in" + '"': `{"in":` N `,"num":{"i":` I `,"pre":` P `}}\n`  |  `{"in":` N `,"str":{"pre":` P `,"str":"` … `"}}\n`
    "il": the four level shapes
    anything else (thm, def, inductive, opaque, axiom, quot, meta) or ANY mismatch above:
          hand the whole line to a generic JSON object parser (any key order, whitespace, all escapes)
          and build the same record from the parsed object. That path sees ≤ 1 % of the lines.
~}

\ul{
\li{app + lam + forallE + names + const + letE + proj cover 99.1 \% of the lines of init and std.}
\li{Strings on the fast path: scan to the closing \code{\verb~|"~}; if a \code{\verb~|\~} is met, either decode the four writer escapes or fall back. Keep name components and string literals as UTF-8 byte ranges in a side pool; a \code{\verb~|java.lang.String~} is needed only for diagnostics. Kernel-special names (\code{\verb~|Nat~}, \code{\verb~|Nat.add~}, …, \code{\verb~|String.ofList~}, \code{\verb~|Char.ofNat~}, \code{\verb~|Quot~}, \code{\verb~|Eq~}, \code{\verb~|eagerReduce~}, …) are found by comparing interned name structure once.}
\li{Do not store binder names, binderInfo, \code{\verb~|nondep~}, or \code{\verb~|all~}/\code{\verb~|isUnsafe~} of non-inductive declarations.}
\li{Integer parsing: ids have ≤ 9 digits on mathlib; nano-lean parses 8 digits per 64-bit word (SWAR) and matches literal key fragments by slice comparison, with a JSON fallback (\code{\verb~|nano-lean-v0.4.2/src/import/scan.rs:57-140~}, \code{\verb~|…/json.rs~}).}
\li{Since ids are sequential, the id field of a record carries no information on the fast path; it only has to be compared with the counter.}
\li{Budget: 6.33 GB at k instructions/byte = 6.3·k G instructions; at the leader's total of 62 instr/byte, parsing should stay near or below 10 instr/byte (≈ 500 instructions per \code{\verb~|app~} line).}
\li{Storage options given §2.12: (a) keep all expression records in flat \code{\verb~|int~} arrays (app = 2 ints; roughly 1–1.5 GB for mathlib) and never free; (b) check each declaration when its line arrives and recycle the records introduced since the previous declaration — only sound with last-use information, because ~5 \% of proof-only records are referenced by later declarations (a pre-pass over the file doubles the scan). What the leaders say about themselves (checker descriptions in \code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena/checkers/*.yaml~}; their code was not examined for this report): kefir "Declarations are checked as they stream in and their terms are freed once checked"; gruszka/pestka convert the export to the binary Susz form first (\code{\verb~|susz pack~}, inside the measured command) and read a theorem's proof block only when the theorem is checked; palladium "The export is parsed in parallel as it is read, in bounded pieces, so the file is never in memory as a whole".}
\li{Expression depth: recursion over records must not use the Java stack naively (\code{\verb~|app-lam~} nests 19,999 deep; nanoda needed its thread stacks raised to 256 MiB for some tests; palladium fails six perf tests with "recursion depth exceeded", logos-kernel declines mathlib for the same reason).}
}
}
}

