\p{Common theme: recursors (especially auxiliary recursors of nested inductives) compared up to defeq or not at all. Most declined/errored/timed-out non-either tests: \code{\verb~|bugs/nat-shift-huge~} (11), \code{\verb~|mathlib~} (11), \code{\verb~|perf/proj-applied-struct-bad~} (10), \code{\verb~|cedar~} (10), \code{\verb~|perf/unfold-before-literal-fallback~} (10), \code{\verb~|perf/proj-applied-struct-arg-bad~}, \code{\verb~|unfold-before-literal-bad~}, \code{\verb~|tutorial/140~}, \code{\verb~|141~} (9 each), \code{\verb~|perf/magma-string-pair-n9~}, \code{\verb~|proj-app-lazy-delta~}, \code{\verb~|proj-applied-struct~}, \code{\verb~|gf3-matrix-product~}, \code{\verb~|unfold-before-literal~}, \code{\verb~|con-leche~} (8 each).}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 Policies of the leaders}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Axioms.} nano-lean, gruszka, pestka, rpylean, palladium, official: no axiom filter in the run command, cedar accepted. sokonanoda \code{\verb~|--axiom-allow-all~}; kefir \code{\verb~|--axiom-policy=allow-all-declared-axioms~} (prints the assumed axioms; mathlib: propext, Quot.sound, Classical.choice, sorryAx); nanoda/nanobruijn/nanoclo/mathgraph config \code{\verb~|"unsafe_permit_all_axioms": true~}. Only con-leche/con-ron/con-agua decline unknown axioms. \code{\verb~|sorryAx~} is an ordinary axiom in init, std, cslib, mathlib, cedar.}
\li{\strong{native_decide / reduceBool.} At Lean 4.36 \code{\verb~|native_decide~} leaves a plain axiom per use (cedar). The strings \code{\verb~|trustCompiler~}, \code{\verb~|ofReduceBool~}, \code{\verb~|reduceBool~}, \code{\verb~|reduceNat~} have 0 occurrences in init.ndjson and std.ndjson, and \code{\verb~|lean4/src/kernel/~} contains only \code{\verb~|reduce_nat~} (literal arithmetic). tenet-jvm states it does not evaluate \code{\verb~|Lean.reduceBool~}/\code{\verb~|reduceNat~} and rejects proofs needing them; tenet (.NET) declines "a file that asks it to trust compiled code". For exports of older Lean versions these names may exist as ordinary constants/axioms; treating them as opaque is sound. Not verified: whether any current arena test contains them (the three tests exported with 4.34.x declare only the three standard axioms).}
\li{\strong{Nat literal extension.} Mandatory (\code{\verb~|other/nat-lit-*~}, gf3, magma, reachability). 4.36 kernel list (\code{\verb~|type_checker.cpp:668-700~}): \code{\verb~|Nat.succ~}, \code{\verb~|add~}, \code{\verb~|sub~}, \code{\verb~|mul~}, \code{\verb~|pow~}, \code{\verb~|gcd~}, \code{\verb~|mod~}, \code{\verb~|div~}, \code{\verb~|beq~}, \code{\verb~|ble~}, \code{\verb~|land~}, \code{\verb~|lor~}, \code{\verb~|xor~}, \code{\verb~|shiftLeft~}, \code{\verb~|shiftRight~}; arguments are whnf'd and must both be literals (or \code{\verb~|Nat.zero~}). nanoda-family checkers enable it by flag (\code{\verb~|nat_extension~}, \code{\verb~|string_extension~}).}
\li{\strong{Malformed input.} official: any reader error is an uncaught exception → exit 1 (rejected). gruszka/pestka wrapper: converter says invalid → reject; cannot convert (limit, unknown record or key) → decline; anything else incl. signals → decline (\code{\verb~|gruszka/scripts/arena-run~}). nano-lean: rejected → 1, budget exhaustion/unsupported/internal → 2 (\code{\verb~|nano-lean-v0.4.2/src/term/outcome.rs:55-60~}); an unsafe/partial definition is "not permitted in a safe proof export" → reject at import (\code{\verb~|src/import/json.rs:270-279~}). palladium: unsupported format → decline, internal fault → 3, I/O → 4. tenet-jvm: verdict starts as error; exit 3 for internal error/unreadable input; never declines. nanoda's arena wrapper maps every failure except 2 to 1. No arena test contains syntactically invalid JSON.}
\li{\strong{Init.Prelude.} README: declarations of \code{\verb~|Init.Prelude~} in a test are either absent or those of an official release; checkers may check more but are not expected to handle foreign preludes; the Lean version in the header may be used. Leaders: gruszka "treats the whole file, including the prelude, as untrusted … builds the quotient package and every recursor itself"; con-ron/con-agua embed con-leche's prelude pins and \code{\verb~|natOpPinSets~}; nano-lean has a check of the primitives it reduces by name (\code{\verb~|nano-lean-v0.4.2/src/import/primitives.rs~}, only skimmed) and can compare the prelude with a trusted export (\code{\verb~|--prelude~} option, \code{\verb~|src/import/prelude.rs~}; not used in the arena command). Tests that redefine prelude names with forged data exist (\code{\verb~|bugs/nat-rec-rules~}, \code{\verb~|nat-rec-k-lie~}, \code{\verb~|unit-rec-rule~}, \code{\verb~|quot-bad-type~}) and are all caught by re-deriving recursors/quot.}
\li{\strong{Resource exhaustion} is reported as decline or error by every leader, never as reject (a reject on a good test costs rank).}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Local reproduction and ground truth}

\ul{
\li{Arena data: \code{\verb~|work/arena-site/{index.html,results.json,lean-arena-tests.tar.gz}~}; tests extracted to \code{\verb~|work/arena-site/tests/{good,bad}/<group>/<name>.ndjson~} (138 good, 92 bad). Links on the site: \code{\verb~|https://arena.lean-lang.org/lean-arena-tests.tar.gz~}, \code{\verb~|https://arena.lean-lang.org/results.json~}.}
\li{\p{Real corpora reproduced locally (Lean 4.36.0 toolchain already installed under \code{\verb!!|~/.elan!!}):}
\pre{\verb~|
TC=$HOME/.elan/toolchains/leanprover--lean4---v4.36.0
LEAN_SYSROOT=$TC PATH=$TC/bin:$PATH $TC/bin/leanexport Init > init.ndjson         # 12 s, sha256 93b61a43…30ba = arena `init`
# std: needs a non-`module` file that imports Std, exactly like the arena's leanfile flow
printf -- '-- Import Std module to make all its declarations available for export\nimport Std\n' > Test.lean
LEAN_SYSROOT=$TC PATH=$TC/bin:$PATH $TC/bin/lean -o Test.olean Test.lean
LEAN_SYSROOT=$TC PATH=$TC/bin:$PATH LEAN_PATH=$PWD $TC/bin/leanexport Test > std.ndjson   # 17 s, sha256 15b659c1…2f7d = arena `std`
~}
\p{Results are in \code{\verb~|work/arena-site/generated/{init,std}.ndjson~}. \code{\verb~|leanexport Std~} directly gives a different, smaller file (557,816,663 bytes); without \code{\verb~|LEAN_SYSROOT~} the binary picks up the default toolchain and fails with "incompatible header".}}
\li{Official verdicts: \code{\verb~|$TC/bin/leanchecker --from-export FILE~} → exit 0 "Lean default kernel accepts the solution" / exit 1 "… rejects the solution: while replaying declaration 'X': (kernel) …". Run for this report on all 92 bad tests (all exit 1) and 5 static corner cases. The arena's ranked "official" is v4.35.0; the tests are exported by v4.36.0.}
\li{mathlib, cslib, cedar, con-leche, navier-stokes-euler, reachability are not downloadable; they need the respective repositories at the pinned revisions plus \code{\verb~|lake exe cache get~} (YAMLs in \code{\verb~|lean-kernel-arena/tests/~}).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Not verified / open}

\ol{
\li{Whether the arena's instruction count is user-only or user+kernel (§1.2).}
\li{Version of \code{\verb~|jdk~} in nixpkgs \code{\verb~|nixos-26.05~}; whether a downloaded GraalVM (or a native image built at build time) is acceptable to the arena maintainers — nothing in the README forbids it; other checkers download toolchains and data in \code{\verb~|build~}.}
\li{Record-kind distribution, longest line, longest \code{\verb~|natVal~}, deepest expression of the \strong{mathlib} export (only totals are known); the estimates in §2.12 are extrapolated from init/std.}
\li{Absence of \code{\verb~|unsafe~}/\code{\verb~|partial~} definitions and of \code{\verb~|mdata~} in the six corpora that could not be inspected rests on indirect evidence (§2.7) and on the writer's code.}
\li{\code{\verb~|String.ofList~}/\code{\verb~|Char.ofNat~} as the literal-expansion constants was read from the 4.36.0 kernel. All 12 tarball files that contain a \code{\verb~|strVal~} are 4.36.0 exports, so the hand-crafted 4.29.1-labelled tests do not depend on it; whether the two corpora exported with Lean 4.34.x (navier-stokes-euler, reachability) contain string literals, and under which constant name their Lean version expands them, was not checked (all leaders accept both).}
\li{\code{\verb~|max_rss~} semantics on the runner (largest process vs. sum) follow from GNU time/Linux documentation, not from an experiment.}
\li{The exact kernel code path behind each official error message in §3 was not traced; the messages are quoted as printed.}
\li{How noisy instruction counts are between runs of a JIT-compiled checker is unknown; the arena measures once per (checker, test) source-hash pair.}
}
}
